thor_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in v4.6-stable and v4.5.1-stable\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.6.stable - Windows 11 (build 26100) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 Laptop GPU (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.8115) - 11th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-11800H @ 2.30GHz (16 threads) - 15.83 GiB memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nThe Angular Velocity property in GPUParticles2D's process material (under \"Animated Velocity\") does not work as expected. Firstly, setting a constant angular velocity causes particles to rotate in the wrong direction. (E.g., positive angular velocity causes particles to rotate counterclockwise)\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/35839626-162c-42fe-ad5e-bb01a600a83f\n\nFurthermore, applying a velocity curve behaves very strangely. A simple curve meant to decelerate angular velocity to 0 instead causes particles to briefly rotate in the opposite direction before coming to a complete stop.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e6c7fdd6-de2b-4b94-ba94-76577ef11ea7\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nSee above and MRP below\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[gpu_particles_angular_velocity_mrp.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/24924182/gpu_particles_angular_velocity_mrp.zip)","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/gists{/